    "reason": "The Japanese expression 形式 (keishiki) primarily refers to 'form,' 'format,' or 'formality.' While JMdict glosses include 'manner,' it is too narrow to represent the general English lemma 'manner,' which broadly encompasses 'way of behaving' (e.g., 態度, taido) or 'method/way of doing something' (e.g., 方法, houhou; やり方, yarikata). Therefore, 形式 is not a natural or comprehensive translation for a general language learner seeking to express the full range of meanings of 'manner' in daily life.",
